You should have a better AC.  There's a whole lot of spoilers and FAQs
at http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/.  Sadly, I don't seem to find
the "What should I wish for" page.  Anyway, many people consider the
following to be part of their ascension kit (AK):

gray or silver dragon scale mail (the former gives magic resistance,
the latter reflection)
fixed boots of speed
fixed gauntlets of power or dexterity
fixed helm of telepathy or brilliance
fixed cloak of magic resistance, fixed elven cloak, fixed robe, fixed
cloak of displacement, fixed cloak of protection, or fixed oilskin
cloak
fixed T-shirt or Hawaiian shirt

Many people like to have a shield of reflection if you are planning to
use a shield.  Otherwise, you can practice your #twoweaponing skill
and wield an artifact in one hand and a highly enchanted non-artifact
in the other (only works with 2 one-handed weapons, obviously).

You can safely wish for +2 items, you can wish for +3 items with about
66% chance of success (failure resulting in +0).  Likewise you can
wish for 2 stackable items (scrolls, potions, etc.) without fail.  The
exceptions are up to 7 candles and up to 20 missiles (arrows, bolts,
darts, IIRC).  Wishing for more than 2/+2 leads to rapidly diminishing
returns.

Rings of preference seem to be free action, slow digestion, increase
damage, conflict (be careful with this one).

If you don't already have one, an uncursed bag of holding will reduce
the weight of its contents by about 1/2; a blessed bag of holding by
3/4.  However, you cannot put another bag of holding, a charged bag of
tricks or a charged wand of cancellation in a bag of holding without
taking extraordinary measures.

Amulets of life saving are quite popular amongst newer players. 
Amulets of reflection work well if you forego the use of any
reflecting armor.  As you note below, magic markers are also very
nice.

I've explicitly omitted any advice about wishing for artifacts as I
never do.  Also, depending on the number of artifacts currently in the
game, and the relative similarity between your alignment and that of
certain artifacts, you may well end up wasting a wish.  You can have
the case that there are too many artifacts in the game for the wish to
succeed, and get nothing, or you can wish for the quest artifact of
another class, only to find out that its alignment doesn't match
yours.  In this case, it will forever evade your grasp (unless you
change your alignment to match that of the artifact).

> And magic marker, I guess if you can't cast spells you can't write
> them. I made one cursed.

Magic markers can be used to write any scroll you have already
identified, or any spellbook you have already identified.  There is a
chance you can write unidentified scrolls/spellbooks, but unless you
are playing a wizard, it's not a great idea.  Dip your magic markers
into a potion of holy water.  A blessed magic marker writing on a
blessed or uncursed scroll will produce a blessed scroll.  A blessed
magic marker writing on a cursed scroll will produce an uncursed
scroll.

Bless your magic marker, then write on uncursed/blessed scrolls of
blank paper (or unlabeled scrolls) to create scrolls of enchant weapon
or enchant armor.  You can successfully enchant armor that is at +3 or
lower (+5 or lower for elven armor and corunathums, latter only if you
are playing a wizard).  Likewise, you can successfully enchant any
weapon that is currently +5 or lower.  Above these limits, you run the
risk of vaporizing the item.

> And also, what is the best place to identify item properties?

If you mean identifying the enchantments on weapons/armor, charges in
wands, etc., you'll want scrolls of identify, or the ability to
successfully cast the spell of identify (requires a good INT, a
spellbook of identify, which must be successfully read, good skill at
divination spells, and a significant lack of metal armor).  Blessed
scrolls of identify have a reasonable chance of identifying every item
in your main inventory (but not items in bags and sacks), and
otherwise will identify 1-4 items (20% chance of each outcome).

